 :prearmed-watchdog
 {:owner "Existing USER systemd manager, independent of every counted Python instruction. ONE transient Type=oneshot main process holds the service in activating/start until it exits. Explicit finite TimeoutStartUSec=29900000 covers its entire lifetime, including first exec/import/open and role placement; TimeoutStartFailureMode=kill sends FinalKillSignal=SIGKILL immediately to KillMode=control-group. There are no pre/post commands or Notify READY messages. Startup completion happens only on main exit; there is no second 30s runtime period."
  :why-not-runtime-max "Installed systemd.service manual explicitly says RuntimeMaxSec has no effect on Type=oneshot. Do not set/claim RuntimeMaxSec as this guard. TimeoutStartSec defaults to disabled for oneshot, which is why the explicit 29.9s value and readback are mandatory."
  :initial-arming "The manager's oneshot start timeout is the independent initial dispatch owner; it must cover spawn/exec itself, not wait for a Python ready callback. Supported startup-timeout semantics are documented in installed v255 manuals; actual pre-exec/stall-before-ready behavior is a named qualification gate below, not a result claimed from merely reading docs. On failure of that fixture, selected allocator is unavailable and live admission stays held; no alternate mechanism is tried."
  :clock-handoff "Parent captures monotonic anchor A immediately before its StartTransientUnit submission and passes A and fixed duration as immutable launch-context metadata on the inherited input pipe. Bootstrap never chooses a new t0. It obtains the exact own-unit ExecMainStartTimestampMonotonic E through the trusted parent readback, and checks kernel PID/start against it with documented tick quantization. Manager's independently armed start timer predates counted exec, and its 29.9s dispatch envelope must be verified relative to E in qualification. Store A and E separately: A is conservative application anchor, E is manager main-spawn timestamp, neither is falsely described as the unpublished exact internal timer-arm instant."
  :overlap "Application deadline is min(A+30s,E+29.9s) on CLOCK_MONOTONIC, established once from the immutable frame; timer begins before any imports even though Python consumes frame later. Initial imports are bounded by manager while application loop is unavailable. Late/missing frame => no GO; manager kills. Manager timer is NEVER disarmed/reset/extended, even after supervisor reports ready, while application timers may only expire earlier. An earlier parent anchor may shorten the allowed work, never extend counted startup. Manager/bus preparation before first counted child creation is not secretly shifted into an unmonitored child startup interval."
  :scope-of-30s "From first counted client spawn onward, initial interpreter/import/source/PNG/resource/identity/store work, every case and settlement all fit the same <=30s envelope. Actual manager dispatch uses 29.9s to leave 0.1s normal scheduling margin; accepted normal-Linux dispatch semantics still apply. Preexisting manager/parent preparation is control plane, not new child work. No new helper is omitted from memory/pids or time accounting."
  :readback "Require Type=oneshot, Restart=no, TimeoutStartUSec=29900000, TimeoutStartFailureMode=kill, TimeoutStopUSec=100000, TimeoutStopFailureMode=kill, KillMode=control-group, KillSignal/FinalKillSignal=9, SendSIGKILL=true, no runtime extension/notify machinery, correct InvocationID/MainPID/E and exact properties before worker entry. Parent readback is not itself the initial guard: manager must enforce configured properties before spawning, as established by no-network qualification."
  :stage "Supervisor enforces per-case min(5s, remaining absolute envelope), including bracket/resource checks, durable intent, POST and durable settlement; never waits on writer fsync. At most six sequential transactions; no later SEND after expiry, failure or uncertain acknowledgement. Stored default unknown remains valid if manager kills all roles before final fsync."
  :normal-clean-exit "Main exits once roles are done/terminated, without sending READY; manager success/failure is recorded separately from durable batch status. Starting main exit/stop is not a second batch attempt. D-state/reaping/server-inference uncertainty is already settled and not an open planning question."}

